<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warwick</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Warwick is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwickshire">Warwickshire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">English Midlands</a>, on the River Avon. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians</a>, fortified it as a burh in 914 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> built a castle there in 1068. Warwick Castle became the seat of the powerful earls of Warwick and is one of the best-known medieval castles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warwick.jpg" alt="Warwick" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: habiloid · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Mary%27s_Church_seen_from_Warwick_Castle,_geograph_6819513_by_habiloid_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward IV defeated the Lancastrian army in fog at Barnet, and Richard Neville, Earl of <b>Warwick</b>, was killed in the rout.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Montagu was the younger brother of Richard Neville, Earl of <b>Warwick</b>, the Kingmaker.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching up the Peninsula from Fort Monroe toward Richmond, George B. McClellan's Army of the Potomac met John B. Magruder's small Confederate force behind the <b>Warwick</b> Line at Yorktown and settled in for a siege, which lasted until 4 May.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near the River Nene, Richard Neville, Earl of <b>Warwick</b>, and Edward, Earl of March, defeated the Lancastrian army of King Henry VI and took the king prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard Neville, Earl of <b>Warwick</b>, landed at Sandwich with a rebel army and the Earls of Salisbury and March, and the three earls rode to London.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The boy pretender Lambert Simnel, who claimed to be Edward Plantagenet, Earl of <b>Warwick</b>, was crowned Edward VI in Christ Church Cathedral, Dublin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
